Looking into the bottom terminal as drawn, \(M_1\) is a common‑gate transistor whose drain is loaded by the small‑signal resistance seen at node \(X\), i.e. the resistance looking into the source of \(M_2\).

Assume both transistors are in saturation and the bias voltages \(V_{b1},V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Neglect body effect for now.

### 1. Resistance seen at node \(X\)

For \(M_2\), gate and drain are AC‑grounded, so it is a common‑gate device with zero drain load:

\[
R_X = \frac{r_{o2}}{1+g_{m2}r_{o2}}
      \;\approx\; \frac{1}{g_{m2}}
\]

### 2. Resistance looking into the source of \(M_1\)

A common‑gate transistor with drain load \(R_X\) has source‑input resistance

\[
R_{\text{out}}=\frac{r_{o1}+R_X}{1+g_{m1}r_{o1}}
\]

Substituting \(R_X\):

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
\frac{r_{o1}+\dfrac{r_{o2}}{1+g_{m2}r_{o2}}}
     {1+g_{m1}r_{o1}}
}
\]

For \(g_m r_o \gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
\approx
\frac{1}{g_{m1}} + \frac{1}{g_{m1}g_{m2}r_{o1}}
\approx \frac{1}{g_{m1}}
}
\]

So the output resistance is roughly \(1/g_{m1}\), only slightly increased by the stacked transistor \(M_2\).

---

**Note on body effect:** If the body terminals are tied to \(V_{DD}\) rather than to their respective sources, replace \(g_m\) with \(g_m+g_{mb}\) for the affected device:

\[
R_X=\frac{r_{o2}}{1+(g_{m2}+g_{mb2})r_{o2}},\qquad
R_{\text{out}}=\frac{r_{o1}+R_X}{1+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}}
\]
